From f49461170b086c2ba9d5a4222403a73573db67c6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ole Algoritme <olealgoritme@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 23:53:23 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] nak: NAK_GPR_CAP, an opt-in register cap that trades spills
 for occupancy

NAK spills GPRs only past the hardware maximum, so a shader uses as many
registers as its schedule needs and occupancy follows: Steel Nomad's big
compute shaders on GB202 use 200-216 GPRs (8 warps/SM), its lighting
fragment shaders 136-144 (12 warps/SM).  NVIDIA's compiler trades spills
and rematerialization for occupancy instead.

NAK_GPR_CAP=N caps the registers per thread of every stage, and
NAK_GPR_CAP_CS / NAK_GPR_CAP_FS override it for compute and fragment
shaders (0 there: no cap for that stage).  N counts registers the way the
hardware allocates them and NVK_SHADER_STATS reports them, including the
two reserved ones, and is rounded down to the allocation granularity of 8;
values from 256 up mean no cap, values below 24 become 24.  The pre-RA
scheduler takes the capped count as its register maximum, so it schedules
for it, and assign_regs spills what does not fit with spill_values, as it
does at the hardware maximum.  An OpRegOut keeps the GPRs its sources need.
The variables are read once per process.

The effective caps are part of nvk_physical_device_compiler_flags (bits
16-39), which goes into the pipeline cache UUID, the shader binary UUID and
the disk cache keys, so binaries compiled under another cap are not reused.
NVK_SHADER_STATS prints the caps in its header when any is set; its gprs,
max_warps and spill columns are those of the capped result.  Unset, NAK
compiles as before.
